Abstract
The positively charged azolium moieties make imidazolium linker an ideal linker for the construction of cationic metal-organic frameworks because the ligand induces cationic environments in the frameworks. Therefore, we employed two imidazolium ligands, 1,3-bis(4- carboxyphenyl)imidazolium chloride $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}^{+}\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\mathrm{~.~}$ ) and 1,3-bis(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)imidazolium chloride $(\mathrm{H_{4}L^{+}C l^{-}}),\mathrm{t}$ o synthesize two nickel azolium-based MOFs, 1 and 2. The as-synthesis MOFs were characterized by PXRD, TGA, FE-SEM, HR-TEM, FTIR and BET measurements. By applying 1 and 2 in liquid phase adsorption of charged molecules of dyes, they successfully exhibit remarkable efficiency for adsorptive removal of anionic dyes, Methyl orange (MO), Congo red (CR), and Orange II sodium salt (OS), from aqueous solution. The framework proves efficient in photocatalytic degradation of anionic dye. Furthermore, in the gaseous phase adsorption, 1 and 2 selectively adsorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ due to the higher quadrupole moment of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Overall, the results show that azolium-based MOFs have potential applications for adsorptive removal of charged organic contaminants from both aqueous and gaseous environment.
Keywords: Azolium based-MOFs; Dye; Gaseous molecule; Adsorption; Photocatalysis
## 1. Introduction
Up till now, environmental contaminants still pose a critical challenging issue worldwide due to the skyrocketing growth of industries like printing, textile, plastic, leather, and medical industries, which generate a huge quantity of byproducts containing dye effluents [1, 2]. The dye effluents are detrimental to the aquatic eco-system and the biodiversity of environment due to their high toxicity and carcinogenic effects on both human and aquatic lives. Unfortunately, they are characterized with low biodegradability and high chemical stability [3], thus arising different techniques for their treatment like electrochemical techniques [4], nanofiltration membranes [5], biological treatments [6], photocatalytic oxidation [7], and adsorption[8],  which is considered as a highly competitive technique due to its wide application scope, economic feasibility, and high efficiency[9]. The inadequate adsorptivity of traditional adsorbent, activated carbon [10], propelled researchers to device an advanced alternative,  metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) whose pores and topological characteristics are tunable by simple pre-design [11].
Numerous strategies are being put into consideration to rationally design and functionalize MOFs to meet the requirements in diverse targeted applications [12, 13]. The remarkable properties of MOFs like high surface areas, large pore volume, pores surface function, host– guest interactions [14], and others are adjustable by rational selection and combination of metal nodes, organic linkers, and solvent together with the synthetic conditions [15]. Therefore, MOFs have demonstrated their potential applications in diverse areas such as catalysis [16], drug delivery [17], bioimaging, chemical sensing, gas storage and separation, and dye adsorption [18]. The adsorptive characteristic of MOFs is enhanced by fabrication of charged frameworks. Unlike zeolites (anionic frameworks) that undergo only cationic exchange [19, 20], MOFs experience both cationic and anionic exchange depending wholly on their charge.  Generally, positively charged MOFs are generated when there is an imbalance of charge between the metal ions and the organic linkers. Nevertheless, to control the charge balance during synthesis in order to design targeted charged MOFs still remains a difficult task. In order to overcome this, a charged organic linker is employed to design the MOFs. Utilizing an azolium linker for MOFs synthesis is an innovative strategy to generate electrostatic environments within the structure [21].
Therefore, positively charged azolium moieties make imidazolium linker an ideal linker for the construction of cationic MOFs. Different synthetic strategies has been reported for immobilizing imidazolium salts into MOFs [22].
These cationic components of the network induce electronic interaction with the guest molecules, thus host-guest interaction is strong, making the evacuation of the guest difficult. Also, these imidazolium moieties generate proton conductivity in the framework [23]. Immobilizing azolium moieties into MOF enhances the selective adsorption of gaseous molecules with different quadrupole moments and polarization. Notably, the higher the quadrupole moments and polarity of the gaseous molecules the higher the interaction with the azolium MOFs, thus the more favourable the molecules would be adsorbed in preference to others [21]. The positively charged framework activates the selective adsorption of ionic species [24]. Consequently, azolium frameworks are useful for the separation of polar organic molecules like polar dyes [25].
Hitherto, there is no report on the use of azolium-based MOFs in the adsorptive removal and photocatalytic degradation of charged organic molecules from aqueous medium. Moreover, employing particular azolium-MOFs in the removal of charged contaminants in both liquid and gaseous environments is still missing in literature. Herein, we report, for the first time, the evaluation of azolium-based MOFs in the adsorptive removal and photocatalytic degradation of anionic dyes in aqueous environment in order to establish the possibility of using these framework materials for the treatment of waste contaminated water. In the gaseous phase adsorption, the azolium-MOFs show selective adsorption of gaseous molecule with higher quadrupole moment.
## 2. Experimental Section
## Material and Methods
All starting materials and solvents were obtained from commercial sources and used without further purification. The synthesis of the azolium ligands, 1,3-bis(4-carboxyphenyl)imidazolium chloride $\mathrm{(H_{2}L^{+}C l^{-}}$ ) and 1,3-bis(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)imidazolium chloride $\mathrm{(H_{4}L^{+}C l^{-}}$ ) was achieved in two steps following reported literature procedures [23, 26] with slight modifications.
## 2.1 Ligands Synthesis
