## 2.2 Synthesis of MOFs
The structural scheme for the synthesis of the two azolium MOFs are presented in scheme S1.
## 2.2.1 Synthesis of MOF, 1
The MOF, 1, was synthesized following a literature procedure [23] after a little modification. 1 $290.79~\mathrm{mg}$ , 1.0 mmol, 4 equiv.) of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}.6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in $3~\mathrm{mL}$ pre-dried DMF was mixed with (86.19 mg, 0.25 mmol) $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}^{+}\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ in a Teflon-lined autoclave. The reaction mixture was heated under autogenous pressure to $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ followed by cooling to room temperature at the rate of $10~^{\circ}\mathrm{C/h}$ . The product was collected by filtration and washed with the pre-dried DMF. Yield: $53\%$ .
## 2.2.2 Synthesis of MOF, 2
The synthesis of the MOF, 2, was achieved following the reported procedure [23]. $(290.79~\mathrm{mg}$ , $1.0\mathrm{mmol}$ , 4 equiv.) of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}.6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in $3~\mathrm{mL}$ pre-dried DMF was mixed with ( $108.19\mathrm{mg}$ , 0.25 mmol) $\mathrm{{H_{4}L^{+}C l^{-}}}$ in a Teflon-lined autoclave. The reaction mixture was heated under autogenous pressure to $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ followed by cooling to room temperature at the rate of $10~^{\circ}\mathrm{C/h}$ . The product was collected by filtration and washed with the pre-dried DMF. Yield: $64\%$
## 2.3 A typical procedure for dye adsorption experiments
$10~\mathrm{mg}$ of the as-synthesized framework was added to a $10~\mathrm{mL}$ aqueous solution of dye $(100~\mathrm{mg/}$ L) under stirring at room temperature, and the solution was further stirred at room temperature for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The mixture was centrifuged, and the plasma was analyzed by UV-Vis absorption spectroscopy after the solution was diluted to 1/10 of the stock solution. The effectiveness of the frameworks to adsorb dye was calculated based on the percentage of degradation $D\%$ which was determined as follows:
$$
\mathrm{D}\%=\left(\mathrm{A}_{0}{-}\mathrm{A}\right)/\left(\mathrm{A}_{0}\right)\mathrm{x}100\%
$$
Where $\mathbf{A}_{0}$ and A are the absorbance of the liquid sample before and after degradation respectively.
## 2.4 A typical procedure for photolysis of dyes
An aqueous solution of dye $\mathrm{40~mL}$ , $10.0\ \mathrm{\mgL}^{-1}.$ ) and MOF $(30~\mathrm{mg})$ were stirred at room temperature for $30~\mathrm{min}$ until the adsorption–desorption equilibrium was achieved. The mixture was irradiated under a 500 W Xe lamp. Aliquots were regularly taken out for UV-Vis absorption spectroscopic analysis to determine the percentage of dye decomposition in water by monitoring the maximum absorption peak of the dye.
## 2.5 Characterization
## 3. Results and Discussion
Field emission scanning electron microscopic (FE-SEM) and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HR-TEM) were used to characterize the surface morphology of 1 and 2 as presented in Fig. 1. The FE-SEM images, Fig. 1 a and b, revealed that both MOFs are composed of a homogeneous surface morphology, with an average particle size of $2.6~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ for 1. The average particle length of 2 is about $3.4~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . The HR-TEM images, Fig. 1 c and d, further indicate the uniform morphology of the as- synthesized frameworks. The energy dispersive Xray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping of 1 indicates the reveal the presence of Ni, C, O and $\mathbf{N}$ , which are evenly distributed in the framework, Fig. 2. Similar result is observed in the EDS mapping of 2 in Fig. 5S.
In self-assembly, framework structures that are most probable to form are those with highly symmetric vertices and ideally one kind of link [27]. The phase purity and local geometry of the as-synthesized samples, 1 and 2, were examined by high-resolution powder x-ray diffraction (PXRD) as presented in Fig. 3. From the spectra, the main peaks appear around $10^{\circ}$ (2Ɵ), 9.80 and 10.77 for 1, and 9.85 and 10.82 º (2Ɵ), for 2 precisely, indicating the formation of highly crystalline material which correlate with MOF crystal structures [27, 28]. Furthermore, closer observation to the diffraction patterns of two MOFs, 1 and 2, shows that the peaks coincides at below 16 and above $30^{\circ}(2\theta^{\circ})$ , indicating that the cell parameters of the two MOFs may be the same.
Thermal stability is a vital feature of MOFs [29, 30], thus thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) experiments were conducted to evaluate the thermal stability of the MOFs, 1 and 2, as presented in Fig. 4. For both structures, the initial weight loss below $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ may be attributed to the release of lattice water molecules and DMF from surface and cavities of the samples. Precisely, for 1, a total weight loss of $12.18~\%$ is observed at a temperature range $78.6\substack{-193.9\mathrm{~}^{\circ}C}$ while a total weight loss of $9.99~\%$ is observed at a temperature range $92.1\substack{-146.7\mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}}$ for 2. A rapid total weight loss of $40.09\%$ and $32.85\ \%$ occurred in the $270.2\substack{-280.5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}}$ and $272.3\substack{-281.9^{\circ}\mathrm{C}}$ region for 1 and 2, respectively and the total collapse of both frameworks was observed at around 400 $^\circ\mathrm{C}$ .
Fig. 5 illustrates the Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra of 1 and 2 combined with the ligand spectra, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}^{+}\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{{H_{4}L^{+}C l^{-}}}$ , of which they are synthesized from. The FT-IR spectra of the imidzolium carboxylate ligands, ${\bf H}_{2}{\bf L}^{+}{\bf C}{\bf l}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{{H_{4}L^{+}C l^{-}}}$ , contains characteristic strong absorption bands for the ν $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{=}\mathbf{O})$ stretching vibration of uncoordinated carboxylate acids at 1703 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1722\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ respectively.  After solvothermal reactions of the ligands with the metal salt, these bands of the uncoordinated -COOH group disappear completely in the FTIR of the assynthesized material indicating the total deprotonation of the -COOH groups in both 1,3-bis(4- carboxyphenyl)imidazolium chloride and 1,3-bis(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)imidazolium chloride. Also, FTIR of the frameworks, 1 and 2, exhibited the presence of two significant peaks at 1577 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1567~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (antisymmetric stretching) and $1318~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and $1313~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (symmetric stretching) respectively. These two new peaks that are absence in the spectra of the ligands are due to the stretching vibration of coordinated carboxylate anions present in the frameworks, thus confirming the coordination between the azolium ligands and $\mathrm{{Ni(II)}}$ for the formation of the MOFs. The broad peaks around $3319~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the spectra is assigned to the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules in the frameworks [23, 31]. The characteristic band at $1172~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is due to the C-H bond at the C2 position of the imidazolium ring [32]. The bands below $900~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ belong to different bending vibration bands of the C-H bonds $(900-800~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C-H}}$ deformation bands of the benzene ring $(780-670~\mathrm{cm}^{-1},$ ).
To evaluate the porosity of the two as-synthesized framework materials, nitrogen adsorption experiment was performed. The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas of MOFs, 1 and 2 are 101.66 and $110.80\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ respectively as presented in Table S1. Similar low BET values have been reported for azolium-based MOFs constructed from a bent azolium ligands [21, 33] and this was attributed to the high interpenetration of these type of MOFs, resulting from the shape and ionic nature of the ligand [22].
The encouraging cationic features of these azolium MOFs [23] inspired us to evaluate their adsorptive ability for organic molecules. Therefore, we conducted a dye-uptake assay of the two MOFs, 1 and 2, with a range of different dyes comprising cationic, anionic and neutral dyes in ethanolic solution. Therefore, ten dyes: electrically positively charged Methylene Blue (MB), Rhodamine Blue (RhB) and Nile Blue A (NBA), and neutral Dimethyl Yellow (DY), Methyl Red (MR), Oil Red O (ORO) and Fluorescein (Fl) and negatively charged Methyl Orange (MO), Congo Red (CR), and Orange II sodium salt (OS), were chosen as model dyes for experiments. The chemical structures of cationic (RhB), neutral (MR) and anionic (CR) dyes are shown Fig. S6. The adsorption behaviours of the MOFs toward these dyes were recorded by UV-Vis spectroscopy. Fig. 6 and Fig. S8 illustrates the different behaviours of the two MOFs in an ethanolic solution of cationic dyes. For MOFs, 1 and 2, the percentage adsorption capacity for MB are $18.34\%$ and $10.81\%$ , RhB are $28.69\%$ and $17.41\%$ , and NBA are $23.97\%$ and $13.29\%$ respectively. The observed percentage adsorption capacity of neutral dyes by 1 and 2; $28.35\ \%$ and $17.80\%$ for DY, $46.62\%$ and $27.03~\%$ for Fl, $13.62\%$ and $10.27\ \%$ for MR, and $48.16\ \%$ and $44.88~\%$ for ORO respectively, (see Fig. 7 and Fig. S9). Furthermore, Fig. 8 and Fig. S10 illustrate that the MOFs, 1 and 2, adsorb up to $75.93\ \%$ and $79.65\%$ of OS, $75.04\%$ and $81.08\%$ of MO, and $97.30\%$ and $98.65\mathrm{~\%~}$ of CR respectively.
Due to the detrimental effect of industrial dye effluents to the aquatic eco-system and the biodiversity of environment, further adsorption capacities of the MOFs were conducted in aqueous medium [34-36]. Notably, neutral dyes were not used in this study because they were not soluble in water. Therefore, the adsorption experiments in aqueous medium were carried out on cationic and anionic dyes.
As can be observed in Fig. 9, the respective cationic dye uptake by 1 was $47.48\%$ for MB, 25.28 $\%$ for RhB and $57.55\%$ for NBA. The percentage absorbance of these cationic dyes by 1 was not so impressive. Similar low percentage absorbance trend was detected when the second framework, 2, was used on these positively charged dyes in aqueous medium.
We proceeded to study the capture ability of the as-synthesized hybrid materials on negatively charged dyes in aqueous medium as illustrated in Fig. 10. Compound 1 was able to capture $87.60\%$ of MO, $79.49\%$ of OS and $97.64\%$ of CR. In addition, high percentage absorbance of $90.37\%$ for MO, $83.11\%$ for OS and $99.87\%$ for CR, was obtained when 2 was examined on the anionic dyes.
The aforementioned results demonstrate that both 1 and 2 have higher scavenging effects on the anionic dyes than on the cationic dyes both in ethanol and aqueous media. The distinct dye uptakes can be attributed to the host−guest (sorbate−sorbent) interaction which plays a dominant role in dye uptake of MOFs [37, 38]. Due to the cationic nature of these frameworks resulting from the positively charged imidazolium moieties in the ligands [31, 39], the as-synthesized frameworks, 1 and 2, presented a higher affinity for the molecules of anionic dyes, MO, CR and OS, while the molecules of cationic dyes, MB, RhB, and NBA are not favoured guests for these frameworks. Furthermore, a low adsorption capacity of the neutral dye molecules, DY, Fl, MR, and ORO, over the azolium-MOFs was observed due to the weak electrostatic interactions between these organic molecules and the frameworks. In all, the maximum percentage of adsorption capacity for cationic, neutral and anionic dyes in ethanol solution is $28.69\%\ (\mathrm{RhB})$ , $48.16\ \%$ (ORO) and $98.65\ \%$ (CR), respectively, indicating that the order of interactions of the framework materials with these organic molecules is anionic $>$ neutral $>$ cationic dyes.
To demonstrate the advantage of the as-synthesized azolium-based MOFs, the maximum adsorption of CR over the 1 and 2 was compared with those of other reported adsorbents as presented in Table 1. It can be observed from Table 1 that the two azolium-MOFs showed a comparable adsorption capacity with respect to other adsorbents. Thus, these MOFs are promising for adsorptive removal of CR and other anionic dyes from contaminated aqueous environment.
The photocatalytic activities [7] of MOF, 2, on the cationic (MB) and anionic (MO) dyes were evaluated in aqueous medium. Before then, the UV/Vis spectrum of the azolium ligand was observed as presented in Fig. S7, illustrating that the azolium linker has a high adsorption band in the UV region which is extended a little into the visible region.  As shown in Fig. 11, the rate at which the MB is degraded is very slow and approximately $55\%$ of MB has been photocatalytically decomposed after 30 minutes of irradiation. For the anionic dye, MO, the rate of degradation was very fast under irradiation of light within 5 min followed by uniform rate of degradation up to $25~\mathrm{min}$ . About $93\%$ of the anionic dye was degraded after 30 min which amount to $40\%$ difference when compared to that of cationic dye under the same time. In the absence of the MOF, the degradation rate of MO was very slow, confirming the photocatalytic efficiency of the azolium MOF.
The reusability evaluation of an adsorbent is of great significant in order to ensure a cost effective adsorbent that has practical application in the industry. The MOF, 2 can easily release the adsorbed MO through simply washing the sample with an excess of ethanol under ultrasonication.  After each cycle, the adsorbent, 2 was centrifuged, ultrasonicated and washed with ethanol for the subsequent cycle. As can be observed, the adsorbent, 2, conserved a good adsorptive capacity of MO even after four cycle of reuse, Fig.12.
As earlier mentioned, the positively charged aromatic azolium component of the ligands generates cationic MOFs. Taking advantage of this characteristic, we tried to study the preferential absorptivity of two gases with different polar nature which can be used for their separation [48]. Therefore, the isotherms of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ were measured for the two assynthesized frameworks as shown in Fig. 13.
As can be observed from aforementioned isotherms, these azolium based MOFs features considerably higher uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ than $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . The lower uptake of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ can be ascribed to its non (quadru) polar nature [21]. On the other hand, preferential adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is due to its high quadrupole moment and polarizability ( ${-13.4\times10^{-40}\mathrm{~C~m}^{2}}$ and $26.3\times10^{-25}\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}$ , respectively) [49, 50], which initiate strong electrostatic interactions with the charged MOFs [51]. Notably, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption−desorption isotherms show a strong hysteresis [51] for both 1 and 2 which is absence in the isotherms of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . Hence, the adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules are not immediately released on reducing the external pressure and are thus trapped within the MOFs because of the strong interaction between $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and the as-synthesized frameworks [52, 53]. The contributing factor for this strong $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -frameworks interaction is due to the cationic nature of the MOFs.
Ideal Adsorption Solution Theory (IAST) was further employed to illustrate the interaction of the azolium frameworks with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . As presented in $\mathrm{Fig~}14$ a and b, the IAST selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ shows a significant higher interactions of the materials with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ at low pressure. The contributing factor for this selectivity maybe because of the positively charged imidazolium moiety in the linker which creates an unusual electropositive environment within the frameworks [51] and the presence of open metal sites in the framework materials. The MOF, 2, has a higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity than 1, due to the lower uptake of the non-quadropole gaseous molecules of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ by 2 than by 1. The result of the selectivity is in accordance with the little higher liquid phase interaction of 2 with anionic dye making it to have a better adsorptive capacity for anionic dye than the framework, 1. Therefore, from the abovementioned results, it can be deduced that the pivotal reason in the preferential sorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is due to the difference in the polar nature of the gases and the charged nature of the MOFs, 1 and 2.
## 4. Conclusions
Two imidazolium ligands were employed to construct two nickel azolium metal-organic frameworks, 1 and 2. The positively charged imidazolium moieties in the linker induced cationic environments in the frameworks. The liquid-phase adsorption of charged dyes over the azoliumbased MOFs was studied to understand the behaviour of dye removal by these materials.  In both organic and aqueous media, these framework materials exhibit a remarkable adsorption of anionic dyes than cationic dye molecules. Under irradiation of light, the rate of the degradation of the anionic dyes by these MOFs is faster than that of the cationic dyes. Furthermore, in the gaseous phase adsorption, the cationic MOFs selectively adsorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ due to the higher quadrupole moment of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . This work illustrates that azolium containing MOFs have a higher affinity for negatively charged dyes than for the positively charged dye molecules. This instinctive characteristic of these materials show that they have potential applications for adsorptive removal of negatively charged organic materials from polluted water and for selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . Hence, researchers should be inspired to immobilize other charged moieties in MOFs for environmental remediation.
